    "taxonId": 82570,
    "summary": "An aquatic fern native across parts of Europe and Asia, from central and southern Europe to Vietnam. Its four-part leaves resemble four-leaf clover and may float in deep water or stand upright in shallow water or on land. In mainland Portugal it underwent severe decline during the last century and was assessed as Critically Endangered.",
    "humanEdibility": "yes",
    "edibilityNote": "The plant has reportedly been used as food in some places for more than 3,000 years.",
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p1", "s1:p2", "s2:p1"],
      "humanEdibility": ["s1:p3"]
    }
  },
  {
    "taxonId": 154116,
    "summary": "A moss found across much of the world, including Europe, Asia, Africa and the Americas. It forms low, loose yellow-green to golden-brown carpets, with strongly ribbed, toothed leaves that are often curved. It grows on soil, rock and rotting wood in wet, lime-rich places such as springs, riverbanks and marshes.",
    "humanEdibility": "unknown",
    "edibilityNote": null,
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p3", "s1:p7", "s1:p8"],
      "humanEdibility": []
    }
  },
  {
    "taxonId": 342005,
    "summary": "A trailing plant ranging from central and southern Portugal through southern Spain and Morocco to Senegal. It has hairy, sand-coloured stems, three spaced pea-like leaves and small yellow pea-like flowers above hairy forked bases. Its leaves range from smooth to hairy depending on location.",
    "humanEdibility": "unknown",
    "edibilityNote": null,
    "evidence": {
      "summary": ["s1:p1", "s1:p2", "s1:p3"],
      "humanEdibility": []
    }
  },
  {
    "taxonId": 154195,
    "summary": "A bryophyte native to every continent except South America and Antarctica. It usually grows in wet soil at disturbed sites, and its capsules mature from late autumn through spring. Its size, leaves and capsule shape vary both across its range and within individual populations.",
    "humanEdibility": "unknown",
    "edibilityNote": null,
